# DurangoRuntime

By [drakolordx7](https://github.com/drakolordx7). Runs Xbox One games natively on Windows: your game's own executable is rebound to a PC version of the Xbox One system layer. Main target is Halo 5: Guardians.

[Download the latest build](https://github.com/drakolordx7/DurangoRuntime/releases/latest)

![Halo 5: Guardians running on PC with DurangoRuntime, mission Evacuation in One X mode](docs/images/halo5-evacuation.jpg)

No game files are included. You need your own dump of a supported game. This is an early preview, so expect crashes and graphics bugs. Not affiliated with or endorsed by Microsoft, 343 Industries or any other publisher.

## Requirements

- Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit
- A DirectX 11 GPU (Terraria needs DirectX 12) with current drivers
- 32 GB of RAM recommended for Halo 5, with the Windows page file left on "System managed size"
- A dump of a supported game, matching exactly:

| Game | Version | State |
|---|---|---|
| Halo 5: Guardians (2018 build) | 1.1.31695.21 | All 15 campaign missions load and play; multiplayer maps offline only |
| Sonic Mania | 1.0.4.2 | Plays |
| Terraria | 4.4.14.1081 | Plays |

## Usage

1. Unzip the download anywhere.
2. Open a command prompt in that folder and run `.\prepare.cmd "<path to your dump>"`.
3. Start the `.exe` in the `playable` folder that prepare creates.

Read the `README.txt` that prepare writes next to the game for controls, settings and known issues. Settings live in `durango_rt.cfg` next to the exe: render resolution, window mode, monitor, refresh rate, frame rate target, mouse, controller prompts and volume. Mouse input is raw. Xbox, PlayStation, Switch and most other pads work through SDL3.

The playable folder holds a rewritten copy of your game's executable. Don't share it.

## Reporting problems

[Open an issue](https://github.com/drakolordx7/DurangoRuntime/issues/new) with the game, your GPU, `durango.log` from the game folder, and any `crash*.dmp` written there.

## Contributing

- Work happens on `main`. Open pull requests against it, and say which game and GPU you tested on.
- Building needs Visual Studio 2022 or newer (or the Build Tools), CMake, Ninja, LLVM (clang-cl, lld) and Python with [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/). Details: [docs/building.md](docs/building.md).
- Some headers are generated from Microsoft symbol data and aren't in this repo. Regenerate them from PDBs of software you own with `tools/gen_xdk_headers.py` before the first build.
- `build.cmd` builds everything into `build\dev\bin`. `tools\make_public_release.ps1` makes the release zip. Runtime settings are listed in [docs/env-vars.md](docs/env-vars.md).

## Credits

DurangoRuntime is made by [drakolordx7](https://github.com/drakolordx7).

Code it builds on:
- XB1Layer by bzai56 (MIT): PE image mapping, the TLS helper approach, WinRT window and gamepad interfaces, D3D11 view rules
- [SlimEra](https://github.com/XWine1/SlimEra) by XWine1 (MIT): kernel, memory and ETW behaviour
- [libHttpClient](https://github.com/microsoft/libHttpClient) by Microsoft (MIT): the XAsync and XTaskQueue task queue
- [libdeflate](https://github.com/ebiggers/libdeflate) by Eric Biggers (MIT)
- DXBCChecksum from AMD's [FidelityFX SDK](https://github.com/GPUOpen-LibrariesAndSDKs/FidelityFX-SDK) (MIT), which includes the RSA Data Security MD5 code
- [RenderDoc](https://github.com/baldurk/renderdoc)'s in-app API header by Baldur Karlsson (MIT)
- [SDL3](https://github.com/libsdl-org/SDL) (zlib) for gamepads, and [FFmpeg](https://ffmpeg.org) (LGPL v3, [BtbN builds](https://github.com/BtbN/FFmpeg-Builds)) for XMA audio, both loaded as separate DLLs
- The prepare tool is built with [PyInstaller](https://pyinstaller.org), [LIEF](https://github.com/lief-project/LIEF) and [pefile](https://github.com/erocarrera/pefile)

Art: keyboard, mouse, PlayStation and Switch prompts from [Xelu's Free Controllers & Keyboard Prompts](https://thoseawesomeguys.com/prompts/) by Nicolae "Xelu" Berbece (CC0).

Research and references (no code taken): [WinDurango](https://github.com/WinDurango/WinDurango), [xvdtool](https://github.com/emoose/xvdtool) by emoose, the [Xbox One Research wiki](https://xboxoneresearch.github.io/wiki/), Wine's xgameruntime, AMD's GCN ISA manuals, LLVM's AMDGPU backend, and the Microsoft GDK documentation.

All third-party license texts are in [THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md](THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md) and in the `licenses` folder of each release.

